\section*{Stable amplitude blowup for a supercritical defocusing torus equation}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Stable defocusing Schrödinger blowup}: The paper claims finite-time self-similar blowup on the twelve-dimensional torus for suitable large odd powers of a defocusing nonlinear Schrödinger equation. Blowup occurs on an open high-Sobolev set and has positive probability for sufficiently regular Gaussian Fourier data.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Stable defocusing Schrödinger blowup}: A smooth profile with a pressurized core is matched to a free exterior, and exact finite arithmetic checks the relevant profile and spectral signs. Outgoing spectral control gives linear decay in the rescaled equation. Nonlinear stability on expanding tori produces an open set of blowup data, after which Gaussian support gives the positive-probability consequence.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Stable defocusing Schrödinger blowup}: The equation is defocusing on $\mathbb T^{12}$, with a selected odd power and a high but finite Sobolev index. The claimed open set has a fixed spatial point whose rescaled amplitude tends to a positive limit, so the conclusion is classical finite-time blowup of the required type.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 371 describes Lean coverage for stable blowup for arbitrarily large selected odd powers and the positive-probability Gaussian consequence.

\textit{Stable defocusing Schrödinger blowup}: The manuscript links \texttt{DefocusingNLS.lean}.

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

